{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ace31014db893b7f3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01R7TMF5jmiorZcKXFYHK85U","status":"completed","output_file":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-sabesonk-Documents-DILMAH-dilmah-engineering-solutioning-poc-vision-segmentation-index-and-retrieval/4eefb16f-3989-4441-9cd3-d294cfccb11f/tasks/ace31014db893b7f3.output","summary":"### Synthesis Agent 1: Gap Analysis\n\nSpec Gaps (Blockers Only):\n- None. Every candidate is resolved by §2.3, §3 or §17.\n\nAmbiguities (Require Clarification):\n- Item: 3b. Does `captions` ever appear as a `why` value?\n  Interpretations: A) `why` is strictly `{\"dense\",\"lexical\"}` as the §7.2.1 bullet literally states, and a captions-only contribution never surfaces in `why`. B) `why` lists every branch that contributed, including `\"captions\"`, per Ordering rule 3's \"why names every branch that contributed\" and rule 1's three live branches (dense/lexical/captions).\n  Recommendation: B.\n  Rationale: The Ordering rules are explicitly marked \"(normative — and score-free)\" and their own examples trail off with \"…\", implying combinations beyond the two shown; the narrower \"why ∈ {dense, lexical}\" bullet reads like a pre-D2 leftover, the same vintage as the stale \"there is no sparse vector, D2\" clause three lines later in the same subsection.\n\n- Item: 4. What sets `capped`/`weak` on Family A tools other than `lookup`?\n  Interpretations: A) The `SearchResponse.capped` field's own definition (\"total > cap\") generalizes per tool: `skim_pages` substitutes `limit` for `cap`; `skim_documents`/`skim_sections` use their fixed ≤10-row internal ceiling as an implicit cap; `resolve` never truncates (\"every candidate\"), so `capped` is always `false` for it. B) `capped` is meaningful only for `lookup`, and is simply always `false` on the other four tools.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: `weak` is already defined tool-agnostically in §7.1 via `WEAK_ABS`/`scope_stats.pages`, in the same paragraph that loosely uses \"cap\" as a stand-in for \"whatever bounds this call\" — the pattern is meant to generalize, only the parameter name differs per tool (§7.1, §7.2.1, §7.2.3).\n\n- Item: 5. Is `fetch` metered against the paid budget?\n  Interpretations: A) No — `fetch` is free; only `read` is paid. B) Yes — the missing \"(free)\" label on §7.2.5's header and the shared fetch/read audit line in §7.4 imply `fetch` also spends.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M4's own header states \"spend: none\" for the milestone delivering `fetch`, and §8.1a's comparison table contrasts `fetch`'s cost (\"the agent's own context, ~1–2k tokens per image\") against `read`'s (\"a Gemini call, the entire paid budget\"); the missing label is a formatting omission and the shared audit line is about tracking image-byte movement, not spend.\n\n- Item: 6. Default answer route — `fetch` (agent looks) or `read` (sub-model looks)?\n  Interpretations: A) §8.1a's bolded \"Default: `fetch`\" governs; §8.2's \"relevant → read\" uses \"read\" as a generic verb (\"go examine it\"), not a literal call to the `read` tool. B) §8.2 is literal, and every `relevant` candidate goes straight to the paid `read` tool.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §8.1a is titled \"Where the answer comes from\" and argues at length for `fetch`-first; the §8.1 loop diagram shows TRIAGE feeding an \"enough?\" gate that branches to `fetch` **or** `read` — a literal reading of §8.2 would make that branch point moot.\n\n- Item: 7. Can F6 close at M2a (§10) when its cited guard I8 is only \"asserted from M6\" (§9)?\n  Interpretations: A) F6 has two independent guards at different layers: the ingest-time fix (`grounded_rate` + reattribution, §6.5's `moved_from`) fully closes F6 at M2a on its own; I8 is cited as a later, second-layer backstop for the same failure class, not a precondition for M2a's closure. B) The catalogue row is simply wrong to claim closure at M2a while depending on I8.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §6.5 \"Code reattribution (F6)\" describes a complete, self-contained ingest-time mechanism with exactly the test named in F6's catalogue row (\"code moved → moved_from recorded\"), independent of any runtime answer-gate.\n\n- Item: 8. Does ingest scale by a work queue or not (§15 Factor VIII vs §15.1)?\n  Interpretations: A) \"Queue claim\" in §15.1 is the run-level advisory lease over `vsir_runs` (D9) — horizontal scaling adds workers that each claim a whole run, never a window, consistent with Factor VIII's \"no window-level queue.\" B) A second, undocumented window-distribution queue exists.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: D9 (§3) and Factor VIII (§15) both describe the `vsir_runs` lease as the only claiming mechanism (\"N runs in parallel... there is no window-level queue\"); §15.1's \"queue claim\" is that same lease at run granularity.\n\n- Item: 9. Is F12 closed at M2a or split (§10 vs §13 M8)?\n  Interpretations: A) F12 is split, matching the pattern already used for F8 and F18: same-revision re-ingest idempotency closes at M2a; cross-revision \"totals don't double when a revision retires\" closes at M8 (\"F12's revision half\"). §10's \"Closed at: M2a\" is simply incomplete. B) F12 is fully closed at M2a and §13 M8's phrase is a slip.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M8 explicitly names \"F12's revision half\" as a separate deliverable tied to `is_current` retirement, and §10 already uses multi-milestone \"Closed at\" entries for F8 — F12's row should follow the same convention.\n\n- Item: 10. How many raster renders, at which dpi, feeding which key?\n  Interpretations: A) Two dpi values, three render sites: dpi 150 only for the embedding raster (step 09, one `Part` in the dense vector); dpi 220 shared by ingest S2 extraction (step 03, feeds `extract_key`) and by `read` (feeds `read_key`, defined as `extract_key` inputs ‖ question) — the \"two dpi-220 rows\" in §4.2 describe the same dpi reused for two purposes, not a duplication; `fetch`/thumbnail renders at 36/72/150/220/300/400 are separate, LRU-cached only, tied to no content-addressable key. B) The two dpi-220 rows are a genuine contradiction.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §6.3's formula `read_key = extract_key inputs ‖ question` makes the reuse explicit; reading §4.2 alongside §6.3 dissolves the apparent duplication.\n\n- Item: 11. §12.3's `lookup(\"EAO 84-5140.0020\") → total 10 (capped:true)` row, with default `cap=20`.\n  Interpretations: A) Treat `capped = total > cap` (§7.1, §7.2.2) as normative; this row's specific numbers are a table-authoring defect to correct once real TC1E-SF data is measured, via C10's own reconciliation process. B) Treat the row as authoritative and infer an unstated smaller `cap` was used for this call.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: With the stated default `cap=20`, `total=10` and `capped=true` cannot both be true under §7.2.2's own definition — an internal arithmetic contradiction, not a corpus difference, so C10's \"recorded rationale\" correction path is the right fix, not a design change.\n\n- Item: 12. §6.7 retirement clause 3 (\"points of any other document are never touched\") has no attached failure row.\n  Interpretations: A) No design gap — both retirement operations (clauses 1–2) are always filter-scoped to `(doc_id, revision)`, so cross-document isolation is structural; the absence is a missing regression test, not a missing guard. B) A real design gap needing a new invariant/failure row.\n  Recommendation: A, but add the regression test.\n  Rationale: §6.7's clauses 1–2 are both explicitly filter-scoped; nothing in the retirement design can touch another document's points by construction — record a cross-document isolation test alongside F12 at M2a/M8 rather than changing the spec.\n\n- Item: 13. `core/verify.py` (§4.1) vs `serve/tools/verify.py` (§4.1) — duplication?\n  Interpretations: A) Deliberate two-layer split: `core/verify.py::verify_claims` is the filter logic (M1); `serve/tools/verify.py` is the HTTP/MCP tool wrapper (M3). B) Accidental duplication.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M1 states this explicitly — \"`core/verify.py::verify_claims`... the tool wrapper comes at M3\" — matching the pattern every other `serve/tools/*` module in §4.1 follows (each wraps a `core`/`ingest` counterpart).\n\nAssumptions (Unavoidable):\n- Assumption: All M2a/M3 (synthetic) work and M2b derivation proceed against the generated PDF and the already-ported `impl/data/raw/` responses, with the real pilot ingest deferred until an operator supplies `TC1E-SF.pdf`. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-1 (§17). | Validation: `vsir ingest data/source/synthetic_3window.pdf --vlm stub` and the M2b parity check against `impl`'s `labels.jsonl` both pass without `TC1E-SF.pdf` present. | Risk: if the file never arrives, the M2b \"once\" real-corpus spend and the acceptance-table reconciliation in C10 (see Ambiguity 11) never happen, leaving §12.3's numbers unverified against reality.\n- Assumption: No live Gemini key is available through early milestones, so `VSIR_VLM=stub` + `VSIR_FIXTURE=<dir>` replay (D10) is the path exercised through M1, M2a, M3, M4, M7, with paid calls gated behind `VSIR_ALLOW_PAID=1`. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-2 (§17). | Validation: `vsir doctor` and the full test pyramid run green with `VSIR_VLM=stub` and no `GEMINI_API_KEY` set. | Risk: behaviour Gemini would actually exhibit but the stub doesn't model (a real S2 schema violation, a real `MAX_TOKENS` truncation) stays undetected until M2b/M5's paid runs.\n- Assumption: Part A consumes the §6.8 export shape over HTTP (and, only if it needs files, from an attached object store), never reads `data/exports/{run_id}` off local disk. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-7 (§17), reinforced independently by §15 Factor VI. | Validation: `GET /runs/{run_id}/export/{labels,observed_tokens}.jsonl` is the only path M8's export sign-off exercises. | Risk: if Part A's real integration needs a filesystem path, this becomes a blocking finding at the M8 hand-off, not before.\n\nStale Text To Flag In The Plan (non-blocking, but a builder will trip on it):\n- §4.2 \"Embedding model\" row — says \"**Text only** — page rasters are not embedded (C12)\" — superseded by C12/D4 (§2.3, §3): the dense vector is confirmed as a fused image+text interleaved embedding, raster included as the last `Part` @ `dpi_index`. Also self-contradicted two rows below by §4.2's own \"Render dpi\" row, which calls dpi_index 150 \"the raster that gets **embedded**.\"\n- §2.2 \"Out of scope\" row \"Sparse/lexical vector + RRF fusion | D2 — deferred behind a measurement\" — superseded by §3 D2 (\"re-decided against the working implementation\"): sparse/lexical ships in v1; only plan2's *measurement* of it moved to an M8 evaluation.\n- §7.2.1 bullet \"`lexical` means a text-index phrase/token hit (there is no sparse vector, D2)\" — superseded by §3 D2, §5.3's VECTORS block, and the same subsection's own Ordering rule 1 (\"lexical (sparse over the extracted text)\"): `lexical` is a sparse vector.\n- §19's C12 traceability row (\"the raster reaches the dense surface through the summary, not through an image embedding\") — already self-labelled \"superseded\" in the row itself; it documents plan2's rejected design, not this spec's.\n- §10's F12 catalogue row (\"Closed at: M2a\") — incomplete relative to §13 M8's \"F12's revision half\"; should read \"M2a (same-revision idempotency) · M8 (revision half),\" matching how F8's row already lists multiple milestones.","usage":{"total_tokens":114436,"tool_uses":16,"duration_ms":540357},"uuid":"cc7c9a22-7027-4bb6-978b-6a726f5d965c","session_id":"4eefb16f-3989-4441-9cd3-d294cfccb11f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R7TMF5jmiorZcKXFYHK85U","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"### Synthesis Agent 1: Gap Analysis\n\nSpec Gaps (Blockers Only):\n- None. Every candidate is resolved by §2.3, §3 or §17.\n\nAmbiguities (Require Clarification):\n- Item: 3b. Does `captions` ever appear as a `why` value?\n  Interpretations: A) `why` is strictly `{\"dense\",\"lexical\"}` as the §7.2.1 bullet literally states, and a captions-only contribution never surfaces in `why`. B) `why` lists every branch that contributed, including `\"captions\"`, per Ordering rule 3's \"why names every branch that contributed\" and rule 1's three live branches (dense/lexical/captions).\n  Recommendation: B.\n  Rationale: The Ordering rules are explicitly marked \"(normative — and score-free)\" and their own examples trail off with \"…\", implying combinations beyond the two shown; the narrower \"why ∈ {dense, lexical}\" bullet reads like a pre-D2 leftover, the same vintage as the stale \"there is no sparse vector, D2\" clause three lines later in the same subsection.\n\n- Item: 4. What sets `capped`/`weak` on Family A tools other than `lookup`?\n  Interpretations: A) The `SearchResponse.capped` field's own definition (\"total > cap\") generalizes per tool: `skim_pages` substitutes `limit` for `cap`; `skim_documents`/`skim_sections` use their fixed ≤10-row internal ceiling as an implicit cap; `resolve` never truncates (\"every candidate\"), so `capped` is always `false` for it. B) `capped` is meaningful only for `lookup`, and is simply always `false` on the other four tools.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: `weak` is already defined tool-agnostically in §7.1 via `WEAK_ABS`/`scope_stats.pages`, in the same paragraph that loosely uses \"cap\" as a stand-in for \"whatever bounds this call\" — the pattern is meant to generalize, only the parameter name differs per tool (§7.1, §7.2.1, §7.2.3).\n\n- Item: 5. Is `fetch` metered against the paid budget?\n  Interpretations: A) No — `fetch` is free; only `read` is paid. B) Yes — the missing \"(free)\" label on §7.2.5's header and the shared fetch/read audit line in §7.4 imply `fetch` also spends.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M4's own header states \"spend: none\" for the milestone delivering `fetch`, and §8.1a's comparison table contrasts `fetch`'s cost (\"the agent's own context, ~1–2k tokens per image\") against `read`'s (\"a Gemini call, the entire paid budget\"); the missing label is a formatting omission and the shared audit line is about tracking image-byte movement, not spend.\n\n- Item: 6. Default answer route — `fetch` (agent looks) or `read` (sub-model looks)?\n  Interpretations: A) §8.1a's bolded \"Default: `fetch`\" governs; §8.2's \"relevant → read\" uses \"read\" as a generic verb (\"go examine it\"), not a literal call to the `read` tool. B) §8.2 is literal, and every `relevant` candidate goes straight to the paid `read` tool.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §8.1a is titled \"Where the answer comes from\" and argues at length for `fetch`-first; the §8.1 loop diagram shows TRIAGE feeding an \"enough?\" gate that branches to `fetch` **or** `read` — a literal reading of §8.2 would make that branch point moot.\n\n- Item: 7. Can F6 close at M2a (§10) when its cited guard I8 is only \"asserted from M6\" (§9)?\n  Interpretations: A) F6 has two independent guards at different layers: the ingest-time fix (`grounded_rate` + reattribution, §6.5's `moved_from`) fully closes F6 at M2a on its own; I8 is cited as a later, second-layer backstop for the same failure class, not a precondition for M2a's closure. B) The catalogue row is simply wrong to claim closure at M2a while depending on I8.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §6.5 \"Code reattribution (F6)\" describes a complete, self-contained ingest-time mechanism with exactly the test named in F6's catalogue row (\"code moved → moved_from recorded\"), independent of any runtime answer-gate.\n\n- Item: 8. Does ingest scale by a work queue or not (§15 Factor VIII vs §15.1)?\n  Interpretations: A) \"Queue claim\" in §15.1 is the run-level advisory lease over `vsir_runs` (D9) — horizontal scaling adds workers that each claim a whole run, never a window, consistent with Factor VIII's \"no window-level queue.\" B) A second, undocumented window-distribution queue exists.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: D9 (§3) and Factor VIII (§15) both describe the `vsir_runs` lease as the only claiming mechanism (\"N runs in parallel... there is no window-level queue\"); §15.1's \"queue claim\" is that same lease at run granularity.\n\n- Item: 9. Is F12 closed at M2a or split (§10 vs §13 M8)?\n  Interpretations: A) F12 is split, matching the pattern already used for F8 and F18: same-revision re-ingest idempotency closes at M2a; cross-revision \"totals don't double when a revision retires\" closes at M8 (\"F12's revision half\"). §10's \"Closed at: M2a\" is simply incomplete. B) F12 is fully closed at M2a and §13 M8's phrase is a slip.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M8 explicitly names \"F12's revision half\" as a separate deliverable tied to `is_current` retirement, and §10 already uses multi-milestone \"Closed at\" entries for F8 — F12's row should follow the same convention.\n\n- Item: 10. How many raster renders, at which dpi, feeding which key?\n  Interpretations: A) Two dpi values, three render sites: dpi 150 only for the embedding raster (step 09, one `Part` in the dense vector); dpi 220 shared by ingest S2 extraction (step 03, feeds `extract_key`) and by `read` (feeds `read_key`, defined as `extract_key` inputs ‖ question) — the \"two dpi-220 rows\" in §4.2 describe the same dpi reused for two purposes, not a duplication; `fetch`/thumbnail renders at 36/72/150/220/300/400 are separate, LRU-cached only, tied to no content-addressable key. B) The two dpi-220 rows are a genuine contradiction.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §6.3's formula `read_key = extract_key inputs ‖ question` makes the reuse explicit; reading §4.2 alongside §6.3 dissolves the apparent duplication.\n\n- Item: 11. §12.3's `lookup(\"EAO 84-5140.0020\") → total 10 (capped:true)` row, with default `cap=20`.\n  Interpretations: A) Treat `capped = total > cap` (§7.1, §7.2.2) as normative; this row's specific numbers are a table-authoring defect to correct once real TC1E-SF data is measured, via C10's own reconciliation process. B) Treat the row as authoritative and infer an unstated smaller `cap` was used for this call.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: With the stated default `cap=20`, `total=10` and `capped=true` cannot both be true under §7.2.2's own definition — an internal arithmetic contradiction, not a corpus difference, so C10's \"recorded rationale\" correction path is the right fix, not a design change.\n\n- Item: 12. §6.7 retirement clause 3 (\"points of any other document are never touched\") has no attached failure row.\n  Interpretations: A) No design gap — both retirement operations (clauses 1–2) are always filter-scoped to `(doc_id, revision)`, so cross-document isolation is structural; the absence is a missing regression test, not a missing guard. B) A real design gap needing a new invariant/failure row.\n  Recommendation: A, but add the regression test.\n  Rationale: §6.7's clauses 1–2 are both explicitly filter-scoped; nothing in the retirement design can touch another document's points by construction — record a cross-document isolation test alongside F12 at M2a/M8 rather than changing the spec.\n\n- Item: 13. `core/verify.py` (§4.1) vs `serve/tools/verify.py` (§4.1) — duplication?\n  Interpretations: A) Deliberate two-layer split: `core/verify.py::verify_claims` is the filter logic (M1); `serve/tools/verify.py` is the HTTP/MCP tool wrapper (M3). B) Accidental duplication.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M1 states this explicitly — \"`core/verify.py::verify_claims`... the tool wrapper comes at M3\" — matching the pattern every other `serve/tools/*` module in §4.1 follows (each wraps a `core`/`ingest` counterpart).\n\nAssumptions (Unavoidable):\n- Assumption: All M2a/M3 (synthetic) work and M2b derivation proceed against the generated PDF and the already-ported `impl/data/raw/` responses, with the real pilot ingest deferred until an operator supplies `TC1E-SF.pdf`. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-1 (§17). | Validation: `vsir ingest data/source/synthetic_3window.pdf --vlm stub` and the M2b parity check against `impl`'s `labels.jsonl` both pass without `TC1E-SF.pdf` present. | Risk: if the file never arrives, the M2b \"once\" real-corpus spend and the acceptance-table reconciliation in C10 (see Ambiguity 11) never happen, leaving §12.3's numbers unverified against reality.\n- Assumption: No live Gemini key is available through early milestones, so `VSIR_VLM=stub` + `VSIR_FIXTURE=<dir>` replay (D10) is the path exercised through M1, M2a, M3, M4, M7, with paid calls gated behind `VSIR_ALLOW_PAID=1`. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-2 (§17). | Validation: `vsir doctor` and the full test pyramid run green with `VSIR_VLM=stub` and no `GEMINI_API_KEY` set. | Risk: behaviour Gemini would actually exhibit but the stub doesn't model (a real S2 schema violation, a real `MAX_TOKENS` truncation) stays undetected until M2b/M5's paid runs.\n- Assumption: Part A consumes the §6.8 export shape over HTTP (and, only if it needs files, from an attached object store), never reads `data/exports/{run_id}` off local disk. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-7 (§17), reinforced independently by §15 Factor VI. | Validation: `GET /runs/{run_id}/export/{labels,observed_tokens}.jsonl` is the only path M8's export sign-off exercises. | Risk: if Part A's real integration needs a filesystem path, this becomes a blocking finding at the M8 hand-off, not before.\n\nStale Text To Flag In The Plan (non-blocking, but a builder will trip on it):\n- §4.2 \"Embedding model\" row — says \"**Text only** — page rasters are not embedded (C12)\" — superseded by C12/D4 (§2.3, §3): the dense vector is confirmed as a fused image+text interleaved embedding, raster included as the last `Part` @ `dpi_index`. Also self-contradicted two rows below by §4.2's own \"Render dpi\" row, which calls dpi_index 150 \"the raster that gets **embedded**.\"\n- §2.2 \"Out of scope\" row \"Sparse/lexical vector + RRF fusion | D2 — deferred behind a measurement\" — superseded by §3 D2 (\"re-decided against the working implementation\"): sparse/lexical ships in v1; only plan2's *measurement* of it moved to an M8 evaluation.\n- §7.2.1 bullet \"`lexical` means a text-index phrase/token hit (there is no sparse vector, D2)\" — superseded by §3 D2, §5.3's VECTORS block, and the same subsection's own Ordering rule 1 (\"lexical (sparse over the extracted text)\"): `lexical` is a sparse vector.\n- §19's C12 traceability row (\"the raster reaches the dense surface through the summary, not through an image embedding\") — already self-labelled \"superseded\" in the row itself; it documents plan2's rejected design, not this spec's.\n- §10's F12 catalogue row (\"Closed at: M2a\") — incomplete relative to §13 M8's \"F12's revision half\"; should read \"M2a (same-revision idempotency) · M8 (revision half),\" matching how F8's row already lists multiple milestones."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ace31014db893b7f3 (use SendMessage with to: 'ace31014db893b7f3', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 116157\ntool_uses: 16\nduration_ms: 540361</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4eefb16f-3989-4441-9cd3-d294cfccb11f","uuid":"7f008538-8939-4d6c-b315-4e2e41696270","timestamp":"2026-09-09T16:58:29.602Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Synthesis Agent S1 (gap analysis) for a PLAN-ONLY implementation-planning task on `development/cr1/spec/spec.md` (repo root: /Users/sabesonk/Documents/DILMAH/dilmah-engineering-solutioning/poc/vision_segmentation_index_and_retrieval).\n\nNine extraction agents have flagged the candidate contradictions below. Your job is to **adjudicate each one** and report ONLY true blockers — things that make a unit unbuildable.\n\nCRITICAL RULE: before reporting a gap, read Spec §2.3 (corrections C1–C12), §3 (closed decisions D1–D12) and §17 (open questions OQ-1…OQ-7 with their default assumptions). Most apparent contradictions are already resolved there. A row in §2.3 or §3 that says \"reversed on evidence\" or \"superseded\" is the WINNER — the stale text elsewhere in the spec is the loser, and that is an Ambiguity to record, not a Blocker.\n\nRead only these ranges (use sed):\n- `sed -n '97,251p' <spec>` → §2.3, §2.4, §2.5, §3\n- `sed -n '1623,1670p' <spec>` → §17, §18, §19\n- plus targeted `sed -n` / `grep -n` on any specific line you need to adjudicate a candidate.\nDo NOT read the whole spec.\n\nCANDIDATE CONTRADICTIONS TO ADJUDICATE:\n\n1. Dense vector composition. §2.3 C12 says (explicitly \"reversed on evidence\") the dense vector IS a fused image+text interleaved embedding, and §3 D4 restates it in detail, and §5.3's VECTORS block lists \"the page raster @ dpi_index\" as the last Part. BUT §4.2's \"Embedding model\" row says \"**Text only** — page rasters are not embedded (C12)\", and §19's traceability row for C12 says the raster reaches the dense surface \"through the summary, not through an image embedding\". Which is normative, and what does an implementer build?\n\n2. §2.2 lists \"Sparse/lexical vector + RRF fusion\" as OUT OF SCOPE citing D2, but §3 D2 rules them IN (ported from impl, captions surface populated, rrf_k=60). Which wins?\n\n3. §7.2.1 `why` bullet says \"`lexical` means a text-index phrase/token hit (**there is no sparse vector, D2**)\", but the same subsection's Ordering rules point 1 and §5.3 both say `lexical` is a **sparse vector** over the extracted text, and D2 ports `sparse.py`. Which wins, and is `captions` ever a value of `why`?\n\n4. `cap` vs `limit`. §7.1 discusses `cap` as if common to all Family A tools and ties `capped` to it, but only `lookup` has a `cap` parameter (§7.2.2); `skim_pages` has `limit` (§7.2.1, ≤10, max 25); `skim_documents`/`skim_sections`/`resolve` have no bounding parameter. What sets `capped`/`weak` on the non-lookup Family A tools?\n\n5. Is `fetch` free or paid? §7.2.1–7.2.4 headers say \"(free)\"; §7.2.6 calls `read` \"the paid step\"; §7.2.5's `fetch` header is unlabelled; §7.4 groups `fetch` with `read` for the audit line; §7.3's quota is a \"read quota\". Is `fetch` metered?\n\n6. §8.2 says triage `relevant` → `read`, but §8.1a says \"**Default: `fetch`**\". Do these name different default routes?\n\n7. I8 is \"asserted from M6\" (§9) but F6's guard cites I8 and F6 is \"closed at M2a\" (§10). Can M2a close F6 without I8?\n\n8. §15 Factor VIII says ingest has \"no window-level queue\" (one worker per run, D9), but §15.1's \"Horizontal scale\" row says \"ingest scales by queue claim\". Which is the design?\n\n9. §13 M2a acceptance lists F12 among \"closed\", but §13 M8 acceptance separately delivers \"F12's revision half\". Is F12 closed at M2a or split?\n\n10. §4.2 has two dpi-220 rows: \"`dpi_answer = 220`, part of `read_key`\" and \"Ingest render dpi `220`, part of `extract_key`\", while §6.1 step 03 renders at dpi 220 and §5.3 embeds \"the page raster @ **dpi_index**\" (150). How many renders, at which dpi, feeding which key?\n\n11. §12.3's acceptance table: `lookup(\"EAO 84-5140.0020\") → total 10 (hits capped at cap, capped:true)` — with cap=20 and total=10, `capped` should be false by §7.1's definition. Is this row internally consistent?\n\n12. §6.7 retirement clause 3 (\"points of any other document are never touched\") has no failure row attached. Is that a gap?\n\n13. §4.1 lists both `core/verify.py` and `serve/tools/verify.py` as net-new. Is that duplication or a deliberate two-layer split (§13 M1 names `core/verify.py::verify_claims` with \"the tool wrapper comes at M3\")?\n\nOutput EXACTLY this format. Be ruthless about the Blockers-only rule: a Blocker means \"no implementer can build the unit without a spec change\". Everything else is an Ambiguity (recommend an interpretation) or an Assumption.\n\n### Synthesis Agent 1: Gap Analysis\n\nSpec Gaps (Blockers Only):\n- Gap: <description> | Impact: <what cannot be implemented> | Location: <spec section>\n  Proposed Addition: <minimal text, ≤2 sentences>\n(If there are none, write \"- None. Every candidate is resolved by §2.3, §3 or §17.\" and say so plainly.)\n\nAmbiguities (Require Clarification):\n- Item: <n. short name>\n  Interpretations: <A / B>\n  Recommendation: <which one an implementer should build>\n  Rationale: <≤2 sentences, citing the section that wins and why>\n\nAssumptions (Unavoidable):\n- Assumption: <…> | Reason: <…> | Validation: <how a unit proves it> | Risk: <…>\n(Prefer the default assumption already recorded in Spec §17 over inventing a new one; name the OQ number when you do.)\n\nStale Text To Flag In The Plan (non-blocking, but a builder will trip on it):\n- <spec location> — <what it says> — <what supersedes it>\n","agentId":"ace31014db893b7f3","agentType":"general-purpose","harnessNoteCount":0,"harnessTailCount":0,"harnessSectionHash":"5312ae45e7a7c8f0","content":[{"type":"text","text":"### Synthesis Agent 1: Gap Analysis\n\nSpec Gaps (Blockers Only):\n- None. Every candidate is resolved by §2.3, §3 or §17.\n\nAmbiguities (Require Clarification):\n- Item: 3b. Does `captions` ever appear as a `why` value?\n  Interpretations: A) `why` is strictly `{\"dense\",\"lexical\"}` as the §7.2.1 bullet literally states, and a captions-only contribution never surfaces in `why`. B) `why` lists every branch that contributed, including `\"captions\"`, per Ordering rule 3's \"why names every branch that contributed\" and rule 1's three live branches (dense/lexical/captions).\n  Recommendation: B.\n  Rationale: The Ordering rules are explicitly marked \"(normative — and score-free)\" and their own examples trail off with \"…\", implying combinations beyond the two shown; the narrower \"why ∈ {dense, lexical}\" bullet reads like a pre-D2 leftover, the same vintage as the stale \"there is no sparse vector, D2\" clause three lines later in the same subsection.\n\n- Item: 4. What sets `capped`/`weak` on Family A tools other than `lookup`?\n  Interpretations: A) The `SearchResponse.capped` field's own definition (\"total > cap\") generalizes per tool: `skim_pages` substitutes `limit` for `cap`; `skim_documents`/`skim_sections` use their fixed ≤10-row internal ceiling as an implicit cap; `resolve` never truncates (\"every candidate\"), so `capped` is always `false` for it. B) `capped` is meaningful only for `lookup`, and is simply always `false` on the other four tools.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: `weak` is already defined tool-agnostically in §7.1 via `WEAK_ABS`/`scope_stats.pages`, in the same paragraph that loosely uses \"cap\" as a stand-in for \"whatever bounds this call\" — the pattern is meant to generalize, only the parameter name differs per tool (§7.1, §7.2.1, §7.2.3).\n\n- Item: 5. Is `fetch` metered against the paid budget?\n  Interpretations: A) No — `fetch` is free; only `read` is paid. B) Yes — the missing \"(free)\" label on §7.2.5's header and the shared fetch/read audit line in §7.4 imply `fetch` also spends.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M4's own header states \"spend: none\" for the milestone delivering `fetch`, and §8.1a's comparison table contrasts `fetch`'s cost (\"the agent's own context, ~1–2k tokens per image\") against `read`'s (\"a Gemini call, the entire paid budget\"); the missing label is a formatting omission and the shared audit line is about tracking image-byte movement, not spend.\n\n- Item: 6. Default answer route — `fetch` (agent looks) or `read` (sub-model looks)?\n  Interpretations: A) §8.1a's bolded \"Default: `fetch`\" governs; §8.2's \"relevant → read\" uses \"read\" as a generic verb (\"go examine it\"), not a literal call to the `read` tool. B) §8.2 is literal, and every `relevant` candidate goes straight to the paid `read` tool.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §8.1a is titled \"Where the answer comes from\" and argues at length for `fetch`-first; the §8.1 loop diagram shows TRIAGE feeding an \"enough?\" gate that branches to `fetch` **or** `read` — a literal reading of §8.2 would make that branch point moot.\n\n- Item: 7. Can F6 close at M2a (§10) when its cited guard I8 is only \"asserted from M6\" (§9)?\n  Interpretations: A) F6 has two independent guards at different layers: the ingest-time fix (`grounded_rate` + reattribution, §6.5's `moved_from`) fully closes F6 at M2a on its own; I8 is cited as a later, second-layer backstop for the same failure class, not a precondition for M2a's closure. B) The catalogue row is simply wrong to claim closure at M2a while depending on I8.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §6.5 \"Code reattribution (F6)\" describes a complete, self-contained ingest-time mechanism with exactly the test named in F6's catalogue row (\"code moved → moved_from recorded\"), independent of any runtime answer-gate.\n\n- Item: 8. Does ingest scale by a work queue or not (§15 Factor VIII vs §15.1)?\n  Interpretations: A) \"Queue claim\" in §15.1 is the run-level advisory lease over `vsir_runs` (D9) — horizontal scaling adds workers that each claim a whole run, never a window, consistent with Factor VIII's \"no window-level queue.\" B) A second, undocumented window-distribution queue exists.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: D9 (§3) and Factor VIII (§15) both describe the `vsir_runs` lease as the only claiming mechanism (\"N runs in parallel... there is no window-level queue\"); §15.1's \"queue claim\" is that same lease at run granularity.\n\n- Item: 9. Is F12 closed at M2a or split (§10 vs §13 M8)?\n  Interpretations: A) F12 is split, matching the pattern already used for F8 and F18: same-revision re-ingest idempotency closes at M2a; cross-revision \"totals don't double when a revision retires\" closes at M8 (\"F12's revision half\"). §10's \"Closed at: M2a\" is simply incomplete. B) F12 is fully closed at M2a and §13 M8's phrase is a slip.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M8 explicitly names \"F12's revision half\" as a separate deliverable tied to `is_current` retirement, and §10 already uses multi-milestone \"Closed at\" entries for F8 — F12's row should follow the same convention.\n\n- Item: 10. How many raster renders, at which dpi, feeding which key?\n  Interpretations: A) Two dpi values, three render sites: dpi 150 only for the embedding raster (step 09, one `Part` in the dense vector); dpi 220 shared by ingest S2 extraction (step 03, feeds `extract_key`) and by `read` (feeds `read_key`, defined as `extract_key` inputs ‖ question) — the \"two dpi-220 rows\" in §4.2 describe the same dpi reused for two purposes, not a duplication; `fetch`/thumbnail renders at 36/72/150/220/300/400 are separate, LRU-cached only, tied to no content-addressable key. B) The two dpi-220 rows are a genuine contradiction.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §6.3's formula `read_key = extract_key inputs ‖ question` makes the reuse explicit; reading §4.2 alongside §6.3 dissolves the apparent duplication.\n\n- Item: 11. §12.3's `lookup(\"EAO 84-5140.0020\") → total 10 (capped:true)` row, with default `cap=20`.\n  Interpretations: A) Treat `capped = total > cap` (§7.1, §7.2.2) as normative; this row's specific numbers are a table-authoring defect to correct once real TC1E-SF data is measured, via C10's own reconciliation process. B) Treat the row as authoritative and infer an unstated smaller `cap` was used for this call.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: With the stated default `cap=20`, `total=10` and `capped=true` cannot both be true under §7.2.2's own definition — an internal arithmetic contradiction, not a corpus difference, so C10's \"recorded rationale\" correction path is the right fix, not a design change.\n\n- Item: 12. §6.7 retirement clause 3 (\"points of any other document are never touched\") has no attached failure row.\n  Interpretations: A) No design gap — both retirement operations (clauses 1–2) are always filter-scoped to `(doc_id, revision)`, so cross-document isolation is structural; the absence is a missing regression test, not a missing guard. B) A real design gap needing a new invariant/failure row.\n  Recommendation: A, but add the regression test.\n  Rationale: §6.7's clauses 1–2 are both explicitly filter-scoped; nothing in the retirement design can touch another document's points by construction — record a cross-document isolation test alongside F12 at M2a/M8 rather than changing the spec.\n\n- Item: 13. `core/verify.py` (§4.1) vs `serve/tools/verify.py` (§4.1) — duplication?\n  Interpretations: A) Deliberate two-layer split: `core/verify.py::verify_claims` is the filter logic (M1); `serve/tools/verify.py` is the HTTP/MCP tool wrapper (M3). B) Accidental duplication.\n  Recommendation: A.\n  Rationale: §13 M1 states this explicitly — \"`core/verify.py::verify_claims`... the tool wrapper comes at M3\" — matching the pattern every other `serve/tools/*` module in §4.1 follows (each wraps a `core`/`ingest` counterpart).\n\nAssumptions (Unavoidable):\n- Assumption: All M2a/M3 (synthetic) work and M2b derivation proceed against the generated PDF and the already-ported `impl/data/raw/` responses, with the real pilot ingest deferred until an operator supplies `TC1E-SF.pdf`. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-1 (§17). | Validation: `vsir ingest data/source/synthetic_3window.pdf --vlm stub` and the M2b parity check against `impl`'s `labels.jsonl` both pass without `TC1E-SF.pdf` present. | Risk: if the file never arrives, the M2b \"once\" real-corpus spend and the acceptance-table reconciliation in C10 (see Ambiguity 11) never happen, leaving §12.3's numbers unverified against reality.\n- Assumption: No live Gemini key is available through early milestones, so `VSIR_VLM=stub` + `VSIR_FIXTURE=<dir>` replay (D10) is the path exercised through M1, M2a, M3, M4, M7, with paid calls gated behind `VSIR_ALLOW_PAID=1`. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-2 (§17). | Validation: `vsir doctor` and the full test pyramid run green with `VSIR_VLM=stub` and no `GEMINI_API_KEY` set. | Risk: behaviour Gemini would actually exhibit but the stub doesn't model (a real S2 schema violation, a real `MAX_TOKENS` truncation) stays undetected until M2b/M5's paid runs.\n- Assumption: Part A consumes the §6.8 export shape over HTTP (and, only if it needs files, from an attached object store), never reads `data/exports/{run_id}` off local disk. | Reason: default assumption recorded at OQ-7 (§17), reinforced independently by §15 Factor VI. | Validation: `GET /runs/{run_id}/export/{labels,observed_tokens}.jsonl` is the only path M8's export sign-off exercises. | Risk: if Part A's real integration needs a filesystem path, this becomes a blocking finding at the M8 hand-off, not before.\n\nStale Text To Flag In The Plan (non-blocking, but a builder will trip on it):\n- §4.2 \"Embedding model\" row — says \"**Text only** — page rasters are not embedded (C12)\" — superseded by C12/D4 (§2.3, §3): the dense vector is confirmed as a fused image+text interleaved embedding, raster included as the last `Part` @ `dpi_index`. Also self-contradicted two rows below by §4.2's own \"Render dpi\" row, which calls dpi_index 150 \"the raster that gets **embedded**.\"\n- §2.2 \"Out of scope\" row \"Sparse/lexical vector + RRF fusion | D2 — deferred behind a measurement\" — superseded by §3 D2 (\"re-decided against the working implementation\"): sparse/lexical ships in v1; only plan2's *measurement* of it moved to an M8 evaluation.\n- §7.2.1 bullet \"`lexical` means a text-index phrase/token hit (there is no sparse vector, D2)\" — superseded by §3 D2, §5.3's VECTORS block, and the same subsection's own Ordering rule 1 (\"lexical (sparse over the extracted text)\"): `lexical` is a sparse vector.\n- §19's C12 traceability row (\"the raster reaches the dense surface through the summary, not through an image embedding\") — already self-labelled \"superseded\" in the row itself; it documents plan2's rejected design, not this spec's.\n- §10's F12 catalogue row (\"Closed at: M2a\") — incomplete relative to §13 M8's \"F12's revision half\"; should read \"M2a (same-revision idempotency) · M8 (revision half),\" matching how F8's row already lists multiple milestones."}],"resolvedModel":"claude-sonnet-5","totalDurationMs":540361,"totalTokens":116157,"totalToolUseCount":16,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14724,"cache_read_input_tokens":78621,"output_tokens":22810,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":18363},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14724},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":22810,"cache_read_input_tokens":78621,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14724,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14724,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":16,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
